PRIVATE REFLECTION: {The deliberation split mainly between A and G. Many colleagues chose A, arguing basket A compounds at 10% while basket B has only a linear 10% drift plus volatility, so A’s expected terminal value is higher; they also invoked volatility drag. A minority chose G, arguing basket B has the same 10% trend but stationary/mean-reverting volatility, so optimal liquidation during year 31 can exploit favorable deviations and raise expected liquidation value above A’s deterministic value. Colleague 9 chose D, treating B’s noise as a mean-zero martingale component with no excess expected return, but D’s “B is a martingale” wording is problematic.

Key unresolved issue: interpretation of basket A. The statement says A has a “constant growth rate of 10% each year” but also “stock price is completely linear with no noise.” If A is truly linear, A and B share the same 10% linear trend, and B’s mean-reverting volatility + optimal liquidation likely makes G correct. If A instead compounds exponentially at 10%, A’s expected value is much higher than B’s linear drift, making A correct. The explicit “completely linear” wording and the emphasis on constant autocovariance and year-31 optimal liquidation point toward G, but many colleagues read A as compounding and chose A.

Important technical points to preserve: B’s “constant autocovariance” implies stationarity/mean reversion, not a pure random walk; thus timing/optionality could add expected value. If B were a pure random walk with drift, optimal liquidation would simply be at the latest point. Martingale answers (B, C, D, F) are false: A has deterministic positive drift, and B has drift plus possibly mean-reverting noise. Necessity answers (E, H) are false because realized B can outperform or underperform. Current leaning: G is likely intended if A is literally linear; A is stronger if A’s 10% is compounded. Need resolve this ambiguity.}

